I hadn't played NetHack in months (gasp!) until this weekend, when
I decided to ascend with a Barbarian.  Well, Cleve I through about
Cleve XXV went down before level 20 due to several annoying reasons,
including a Wand of Death that several dwarves in the Gnomish Mines
seemed to have fun passing around.  Anyway, I hit about level 17
while going strong, went down the steps, and got a message like
"All is not kosher.  Somebody's been messing with the level files.
This game is void."  Went in the high score list as a trickery.

Grrr.  I hadn't been mucking around with the level files, so I 
popped out the unerase utility (NH313386, on a 486 PC) and unerased
all my character's level files (not the .sav file) and then ran
recover and got everything back, except for the extraneous highscore
entry.  Continued on...

Castle level.  Going good.  +6 Vorpie, GDSM (the hard way -- killed
the sucker myself, unknown scroll of enchant armor turned out 
cursed, had to waste the blessed one to get +0 GDSM), GoD, speed
boots, SoR, all the intrinsics except for disintegration resistence
"You feel firm."  (Ooo, baby.), though shock resistence was still
in a ring.  Anyway, hit the trapdoor to Gehannom and got another 
trickery message.  Dammit.  So this time I wasn't quite as lucky 
with the undeleting and level 0 didn't come out, so I lost the
game.  The last .sav file didn't pan out, either. 

Is this a common problem?  Something with file dating or some other
esoterica?  Needless to say, I was peeved.  Time to start backing
up save files, which I hadn't done since recover was fixed for 3.1.2.
Grrrr.

Is there a way to assert "I'm not cheating; don't crash for a trickery"
without dramatic code changes?

Maybe I'll try again in another month.  With an elf or something.
Just not ready for the Elbereth-wimp method yet.

Dave Ripton



